---
title: "09a  External audit by COA: from submission of accounts to the annual reports"
---
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  %% QUESTION THIS DIAGRAM ANSWERS: what happens between the agency and the Commission on Audit (COA) after money is spent, and how does a finding become a personal liability?
  %% IDEA: COA audits after payment. A finding on the accounts as a whole is written in the audit report with a recommendation. A finding on one transaction may become a notice that makes named persons liable to explain, to complete a requirement, or to return money.
  %% SOURCES: COA examines, audits and settles all accounts and sets accounting and auditing rules [CONST IX-D.2]. Annual report to the President and Congress [CONST IX-D.4]. Deadlines of 14 February and 30 September [PD1445 s.41]. Audit after payment since 2011 [COA2011-002]. Notice of Suspension, Notice of Disallowance, Notice of Charge, quarterly statement, notice of settlement, appeal levels, Notice of Finality of Decision, COA Order of Execution [COA2009-006]. Kinds of audit and citizen participatory audit [ROADMAP2024]. No formal scrutiny by the legislature of the audit reports on the annual financial reports (indicator 31, score D) [PEFA2025].
  %% GENERAL KNOWLEDGE, NOT OPENED IN A SOURCE HERE: the Audit Observation Memorandum and the reply (steps 3 and 4), the short definitions of the three notices in steps 5 to 7, the structure of the agency audit report, the action plan, review by the Supreme Court.
  %% NOTE: the 2009 rules also name an Adjudication and Settlement Board between the Director and the Commission Proper for some cases. I did not check whether that board still operates, so it is not drawn.
  participant ACC as Agency accounting<br/>and other units
  participant HEAD as Head of agency<br/>and persons liable
  participant AT as COA audit team<br/>(team leader, supervising auditor)
  participant DIR as COA Cluster or<br/>Regional Director
  participant CP as COA Commission<br/>Proper
  participant PC as President<br/>and Congress

  ACC->>AT: Accounts: paid vouchers, journal entries, reports.<br/>Year-end statements by 14 February
  AT->>AT: Audit: financial (are the statements fairly presented),<br/>compliance (were the rules followed),<br/>performance (were results achieved with economy)<br/>#nbsp;
  AT->>HEAD: Audit Observation Memorandum on each finding
  HEAD-->>AT: Comments, explanations, documents

  alt Finding on a transaction: a requirement is missing
    AT->>HEAD: Notice of Suspension. The amount is suspended<br/>until the requirement is met
  else Finding on a transaction: illegal, irregular, unnecessary, excessive or extravagant expense
    AT->>HEAD: Notice of Disallowance, naming the persons liable
  else Finding on revenue: under-assessment or under-collection
    AT->>HEAD: Notice of Charge, naming the persons liable
  end

  alt Persons liable comply
    HEAD->>AT: Requirement met, or amount returned
    AT-->>HEAD: Notice of Settlement
  else Persons liable contest
    HEAD->>DIR: Appeal
    DIR-->>HEAD: Decision
    HEAD->>CP: Petition for review
    CP-->>HEAD: Decision of the Commission Proper
  end

  AT->>HEAD: When a decision is final: Notice of Finality of Decision,<br/>then COA Order of Execution (for example, withholding of pay)
  AT->>HEAD: Each quarter: Statement of Audit Suspensions,<br/>Disallowances and Charges
  AT->>HEAD: Draft findings discussed. Then the audit report of the agency:<br/>opinion on the statements, observations and recommendations,<br/>status of earlier recommendations
  HEAD-->>AT: Action plan on the recommendations
  AT->>PC: COA annual report on the financial condition and operation<br/>of the government, by 30 September

  Note over ACC,PC: REPORTED WEAKNESS: the legislature has no formal scrutiny of the audit reports on the annual financial reports (PEFA assessment 2025, indicator 31, score D).
